# Registered jobs and schedules

Jobs use `JobQueuePort`; schedules use `SchedulerPort`. The domain and application
layers import neither Taskiq nor Redis. Register handlers in
`app/infrastructure/job_registry.py`; `heartbeat` is a harmless example. Parameters,
Python import paths, expressions and uploaded code are not accepted by the management UI.
All example schedules start **disabled**.

From the generated project after `make up`:

```
harness jobs list
harness jobs run heartbeat
harness schedules list
```

These development commands call Docker Compose with an argument list, execute the
generated application's CLI in the running API container and propagate its exit code.
The application has no dependency on the Harness. Equivalent standalone commands:
`python -m app.infrastructure.jobs_cli jobs list`, `jobs run heartbeat`, `schedules list`.

## Memory

Memory mode runs its consumer and scheduler inside the API lifespan. Queue capacity
is 1000, the last 100 results are retained, and missed intervals coalesce into one job.
State is process-local and lost on restart. CLI runs use their own process: a memory
job is queued and consumed before the command exits; schedule listing shows registered
defaults. Changes in an API process are visible in that process's admin. Use one API
process for this development mode. There is no separate worker container in memory mode.

## Taskiq + Redis

Taskiq uses Redis Streams, a worker process and, when enabled, a scheduler process.
The dedicated `jobs-redis` service has an AOF volume, separate from disposable cache data.
`make down` preserves this volume. Configure `JOBS_REDIS_URL` and `JOBS_NAMESPACE` in
API, worker and scheduler consistently. Credentials use environment configuration;
there is no public Redis port. Production Redis access must be restricted to trusted
application processes. No untrusted callers should be able to publish broker messages.

The worker acknowledges after execution. Delivery can repeat following a crash;
handlers must be idempotent. There is no exactly-once guarantee, automatic business
retry policy, dead-letter queue or stream retention policy in this provisional adapter.
Results expire after one hour. Run **one scheduler replica**; this phase does not
implement distributed scheduler leadership. Interval timing is controlled by Taskiq;
changing or disabling a schedule is checked again before enqueueing. An already queued
job cannot be cancelled by disabling its schedule. AOF `everysec` has Redis's usual
up-to-one-second crash-loss window; it is not a no-loss guarantee.

## Administration

Select `admin.resources: [schedules]` together with enabled jobs, scheduler and auth.
The authenticated `/admin/schedules` page lists only registered schedules. It changes
enabled state and intervals (1–86400 seconds), with CSRF protection and input validation.
In permissions mode the user needs `admin:access`, `schedules:read` and, for mutations,
`schedules:write`; roles mode requires `admin`. Existing `AUTH_ADMIN_SUBJECTS` restrictions
also apply to remote identities. Never add a default password. Local development over
HTTP requires explicit `AUTH_COOKIE_SECURE=false`; HTTPS secure cookies remain the default.

## Verification

`make test-contracts` runs memory contracts and, for Taskiq, real SDK tests with an
in-memory transport. To test the Redis store use `TEST_JOBS_REDIS_URL` when running
`pytest tests/contracts/test_taskiq_jobs.py`. `scripts/smoke_jobs.py` in the Harness
repository additionally starts real Docker worker/scheduler processes, observes queued
and scheduled results, verifies CLI and authenticated schedule management, and cleans up
only its own temporary project and volumes. No external cloud accounts are required.
